{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_06a70a4fe849e4420169bb4214b7888191a6f4a8d7a3266d0b", "created": 1773879828, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Lymph Nodes and Neck-Centered Nodal Disease, Testing, and Cancer Staging\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community is organized around LYMPH NODES as both a core immune/filtration structure and a clinically decisive site for cancer spread, staging, recurrence surveillance, and treatment planning. The NECK emerges as a key anatomical hub because it contains many lymph nodes and is routinely examined (palpation) and imaged (notably ultrasound) for suspected nodal enlargement or cancer involvement. Multiple entities describe how clinicians distinguish local disease from regional lymph-node involvement—especially in cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC)—and how that distinction drives follow-up intensity, imaging, and surgical approaches such as lymph node dissection or neck dissection. The community also covers nodal disease as a primary disease site in lymphoid malignancies (e.g., B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma) and highlights advanced spread concepts such as extranodal extension and distant metastasis (defined relative to the neck in head-and-neck contexts).\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Lymph nodes are the community’s central clinical decision point: filtration, enlargement, and a key site of cancer spread and staging\",\n      \"explanation\": \"LYMPH NODES are described as small immune/lymphatic structures distributed throughout the body that filter lymph and help remove foreign material and harmful cells (including cancer cells). This filtering and immune role is explicitly tied to capturing foreign particles and harmful cells, reinforcing lymph nodes as a biological “checkpoint” where clinically meaningful abnormalities can appear. [Data: Entities (33, 3915,