 548, 549)]\\n\\nThis local/regional distinction is tied to different monitoring and evaluation approaches. Regional disease is linked to more intensive follow-up (e.g., frequent physical exams with skin checks) and additional assessment such as lymph node testing to determine how far the cancer has spread. Follow-up exams and schedules are highlighted as essential after treatment to detect recurrence and new skin cancers, with schedules varying by disease extent and risk level. [Data: Entities (863, 891, 910, 892); Relationships (1246, 1297, 1289, 1352)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"A diagnostic toolkit is centered on lymph node testing and biopsy approaches when spread is suspected\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes “LYMPH NODE TESTING” as a distinct activity used to evaluate suspicious lymph nodes to determine extent of spread, informing staging and treatment selection. This reinforces lymph nodes as an evidence source that can be sampled and tested to confirm or refute suspected metastatic involvement. [Data: Entities (863, 33); Relationships (1246)]\\n\\nTwo biopsy strategies are represented. NEEDLE BIOPSY (LYMPH NODE SAMPLE) is described as using a needle to take a lymph node sample when nodal spread is suspected, while EXCISIONAL BIOPSY is described as surgically removing tissue—often all or part of a lymph node—by cutting into the skin, sometimes used when less invasive methods are not feasible or results conflict with clinical findings. [Data: Entities (434, 3781, 6064, 33); Relationships (546, 13828)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Surgical management focuses on removing tumor plus lymph nodes, with specialized operations for neck/thyroid contexts and nodal recurrence\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Surgery is represented in multiple forms that explicitly include lymph nodes. “TUMOR AND LYMPH NODE REMOVAL” is described as removing tumor tissue and nearby lymph nodes as an important component of pathologic staging and post-surgical assessment. This makes lymph node removal not only therapeutic but also diagnostic for staging. [Data: Entities (2570, 33); Relationships (4714)]\\n\\nMore extensive procedures are also present. LYMPH NODE DISSECTION is described as removal of multiple lymph nodes (more extensive than biopsy), used when biopsy indicates nodal cancer involvement and also used for recurrent cancer in lymph nodes—particularly when additional radiation is not used or not possible. Separately, NECK DISSECTION is defined as removing lymph nodes near the thyroid when they are known or suspected to contain cancer, indicating a specialized head-and-neck/thyroid surgical pathway. [Data: Entities (578, 5022, 33, 6197); Relationships (14252, 11066)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Nodal recurrence is treated as a distinct surveillance outcome that can trigger additional therapy\",\n      \"explanation\": \"“CANCER RETURNING TO LYMPH NODES” is defined as recurrence found in lymph nodes during surveillance, establishing nodal recurrence as a specific, trackable pattern of disease return rather than a generic recurrence concept. This integrates lymph nodes into the longitudinal timeline of cancer care (post-treatment monitoring). [Data: Entities (6197, 33); Relationships (14194)]\\n\\nThe management implications are represented through the linkage between nodal recurrence and lymph node dissection when additional radiation is not used or possible. This suggests that nodal recurrence can lead to renewed surgical intervention focused on removing affected nodes. [Data: Entities (578, 6197); Relationships (14252)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Lymph nodes are also primary disease sites in lymphoid malignancy (B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma), not just metastatic targets\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes B-CELL LYMPHOBLASTIC LYMPHOMA (B-LL/B-LBL) and a parallel label (B-LL / B-LBL), both defined by B-cell lymphoblasts growing mostly in lymph nodes rather than primarily in bone marrow. This frames lymph nodes as a primary site of malignant proliferation in certain hematologic cancers. [Data: Entities (2611, 2914, 3042, 33); Relationships (4784, 5419, 5978)]\\n\\nThis perspective complements the metastatic framing of lymph nodes by emphasizing that lymph node enlargement or nodal disease may reflect primary lymphoid malignancy, not only spread from a solid tumor. The dataset’s explicit “nodal disease” definition reinforces that cancer can be found in lymph nodes as a core diagnostic finding across disease types. [Data: Entities (441, 33); Relationships (547)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Spread terminology distinguishes regional nodal involvement from more advanced patterns like extranodal extension and distant metastasis (relative to the neck)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"EXTRANODAL EXTENSION is defined as cancer spreading outside the lymph node, representing a more advanced local behavior once cancer is already in a node. This concept adds nuance beyond a simple “node positive” status by characterizing the extent of spread around the involved lymph node. [Data: Entities (1700, 33); Relationships (2773)]\\n\\nDISTANT METASTASIS is defined as spread to distant organs and/or distant lymph nodes, and in head-and-neck contexts it is explicitly distinguished as spread beyond the neck. This definition uses the NECK as a boundary marker separating regional disease (within/near neck nodes) from distant metastatic disease. [Data: Entities (1699, 29); Relationships (2780)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Key anatomical clusters (armpits/underarms/axilla) and lymph drainage pathways reinforce how clinicians localize nodal evaluation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Beyond the neck, the ARMPITS and UNDERARMS are explicitly identified as regions where lymph nodes are concentrated and can be examined for enlargement. The dataset also defines AXILLARY LYMPH NODES (ALNS) as lymph nodes near the ARMPIT (AXILLA), reinforcing a common, clinically meaningful nodal basin. [Data: Entities (1226, 3428, 3549, 3550, 33); Relationships (1982, 6874, 7222, 7385)]\\n\\nA functional pathway is also included via LYMPH DRAINAGE, describing movement of lymph from breast tissue through lymph vessels to lymph nodes. This supports how lymphatic anatomy (vessels and drainage) relates to where clinicians expect nodal involvement or where evaluation may be focused. [Data: Entities (791, 3567); Relationships (7218)]\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 4.2,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity is moderate because the community describes clinically significant concepts—nodal spread, staging, recurrence, and invasive diagnostic/surgical interventions—used to guide cancer management rather than indicating harmful activity or misconduct.\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null, "reasoning_content": "**Compiling medical report**\n\nI need to generate a community report focused on medical and oncology concepts. The impact severity is informational, maybe a moderate level due to clinical staging and procedures. I should select a rating around 3.5 or 4.0 and aim for 5-10 findings, structured in multiple paragraphs, totaling under 2000 words. The title should be concise and specific, like “Lymph Nodes, Neck, and Nodal Cancer Spread/Staging.” \n\nFindings will revolve around key entities and their relationships, emphasizing central lymph nodes, neck regions, cancer spread concepts, diagnostic procedures, and surveillance methods."}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 2707, "prompt_tokens": 9790, "total_tokens": 12497, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 454, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}